problems.problem:
Find a sequence of positive integers $f(n)$ ($n\in\mathbb{N}$) such that:

(i) $f(n)\le n^8$ for every $n\ge 2$;

(ii) for any distinct $a_1,\ldots,a_k,n$, $f(n)\neq f(a_1)+\cdots+f(a_k)$.

Let $n\ge 2$ be an integer. Show that there exists a subset $A\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$ such that:

(i) the number of elements of $A$ is at most $2\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor+1$;

(ii) $\{|x-y| \mid x,y\in A,\ x\neq y\}=\{1,2,\ldots,n-1\}$.

Find all sets $A$ and $B$ that satisfy the following conditions:

a) $A\cup B=\mathbb{Z}$;

b) if $x\in A$ then $x-1\in B$;

c) if $x,y\in B$ then $x+y\in A$.

For every positive integer $n$, let $f(n)$ be the number of choices of signs $+$ or $-$ in the expression $\pm1\pm2\pm\cdots\pm n$ such that the resulting sum is zero. Show that $f(n)$ satisfies the following conditions:

a) $f(n)=0$ for $n\equiv 1\pmod 4$ or $n\equiv 2\pmod 4$;

b) $2^{\frac n2-1}\le f(n)\le 2^n-2^{\lfloor\frac n2\rfloor+1}$ for $n\equiv 0\pmod 4$ or $n\equiv 3\pmod 4$.

There are $n\ge 2$ players playing a card game with $np$ cards in $p$ rounds. The cards are coloured in $n$ colours, and the cards of each colour are labelled with the numbers $1,2,\ldots,p$. The game obeys the following rules:

• each player receives $p$ cards;

• the player who begins a round plays a card, and each other player has to play a card of the same colour if he has one; otherwise he may play an arbitrary card;

• the winner of the round is the player who has played the greatest card of the same colour as the first one;

• the winner of a round starts the next round with a card of his choice, and the play continues with the same rules;

• played cards are out of the game.

Show that if all cards labelled $1$ are winners, then $p\ge 2n$.

On an $n\times m$ board, a move is the following succession of operations:

(i) choose some unmarked squares, no two in the same row or column;

(ii) mark them with $1$;

(iii) mark with $0$ all the unmarked squares which lie in the same row or column as a square marked with $1$ (even if that square was marked with $1$ in an earlier move).

A game is a succession of moves that ends when no more moves can be made. What is the maximum possible sum of the numbers on the board at the end of a game?

Show that:

a) there are infinitely many positive integers $n$ for which there exists a perfect square equal to the sum of the squares of $n$ consecutive positive integers (for instance, $2$ is such a number since $5^2=3^2+4^2$);

b) if $n$ is a positive integer which is not a perfect square and $x$ is an integer such that $x^2+(x+1)^2+\dots+(x+n-1)^2$ is a perfect square, then there are infinitely many positive integers $y$ such that $y^2+(y+1)^2+\dots+(y+n-1)^2$ is a perfect square.

Let $k>1$ be a positive integer. Construct an infinite family $\mathcal{A}$ of subsets of $\mathbb{N}$ with the following properties:

(a) any $k$ distinct sets of $\mathcal{A}$ have exactly one common element;

(b) any $k+1$ distinct sets of $\mathcal{A}$ have empty intersection.

For positive integers $k\le n$, let $M(n,k)$ denote the least common multiple of the numbers $n,n-1,\ldots,n-k+1$. Let $f(n)$ be the largest positive integer $k\le n$ such that $M(n,1)<M(n,2)<\ldots<M(n,k)$. Prove that:

(a) $f(n)<3\sqrt n$ for all positive integers $n$;

(b) for every positive integer $N$, $f(n)>N$ for all but finitely many positive integers $n$.

Divide the plane into $1\times 1$ squares formed by the lattice lines. Let $S$ be the union of finitely many such cells, and let $a$ be a positive real number with $a\le\frac14$. Show that $S$ can be covered by finitely many squares satisfying the following three conditions:

1) each square of the cover is an array of $1\times 1$ cells;

2) the squares of the cover have pairwise disjoint interiors;

3) for each square $Q$ of the cover, the ratio of the area of $S\cap Q$ to the area of $Q$ is at least $a$ and at most $a\left(\left\lfloor a^{-1/2}\right\rfloor\right)^2$.

Let $m$ and $n$ be two positive integers and let $A_1, \dots, A_m$ be sets of positive integers such that:

(1) $A_i$ and $A_j$ are disjoint for all distinct indices $i$ and $j$;

(2) $|A_i| = n$, $i = 1, \dots, m$;

(3) For every index $i$, no element of $A_i$ is divisible by any element of $A_{i+1}$, where indices are taken modulo $m$.

Determine the maximum number of ordered pairs $(a, b)$, where $a$ and $b$ are elements of different $A_i$'s and $b$ is divisible by $a$.

For each positive integer $n > 1$, denote by $p(n)$ the largest prime factor of $n$. Determine all triples $x, y, z$ of distinct positive integers which simultaneously satisfy the conditions:

(1) $x, y, z$ are in arithmetic progression;

(2) $p(xyz) \le 3$.
